#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse_jacobi_cn

#% text
# `inverse_jacobi_cn(v, m)` gives the `u` with `jacobi_cn(u, m) = v`, the inverse of the Jacobi elliptic function `cn`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The value is the principal branch of DLMF 22.15, real for a real `v` in the range of the function on the real line and `0 <= m < 1`.
#% item
# For a real `m > 1` the value is `inverse_jacobi_dn(v, 1/m)/sqrt(m)` (the reciprocal-modulus transformation, DLMF 22.17), real for a real `v` in the range of the function on the real line.

#% item
# Machine numbers and balls give numerical values; exact arguments stay unevaluated.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse_jacobi_cn(0.5, 0.5)
#% output
#= 1.1424290580457774

#% cell
inverse_jacobi_cn(1.0, 0.5)
#% output
#= 0.0

#% cell
inverse_jacobi_cn(-0.5, 0.3)
#% output
#= 2.3286436732655393

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `jacobi_cn` undoes it:

#% cell
jacobi_cn(inverse_jacobi_cn(0.5, 0.4), 0.4)
#% output
#= 0.4999999999999999

#% text
# With `m = 0` the Jacobi functions are trigonometric functions, so the inverse is a trigonometric inverse:

#% cell
inverse_jacobi_cn(0.5, 0)
#% output
#= 1.0471975511965979

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `jacobi_cn`, `inverse_jacobi_sn`, `jacobi_amplitude`, `elliptic_f`
